<p>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said Friday in Würzburg that he would not advise his children or other young Germans to go to the United States to study or work, citing a rapidly changed “social climate” and limited job prospects even for highly educated people. Speaking to young people at a Catholic gathering, Merz said Germany offers strong opportunities and described himself as “a great admirer of America,” adding that his admiration was “not increasing” at the moment. The remarks followed weeks of public tension between Berlin and Washington under President Trump over trade, Ukraine and the war involving Iran. Merz said on April 28 that the American president had been “humiliated” by Iran and lacked a coherent strategy, and reports said the Pentagon later announced plans to withdraw 5,000 U.S. troops from Germany. Reports also said Washington raised tariffs on European Union cars, a sector central to Germany’s economy. Merz spoke by phone with President Trump on Friday and later said the United States and Germany are “strong partners in a strong NATO”. FAZ reported that the two leaders were “in agreement” on Iran during the call, while Handelsblatt said Merz’s comments drew criticism from Trump’s allies and that the chancellor wants to explain his political course more clearly.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IDoWzZz8939nfctoGFnyeqgMID1ngbXYnJ3HCI_WYGzoGpGowv_EUVATJenEYAcyJ6P7xFv7Iy91DATi-ybKKH3lqrThR6H1FjJoQfWAfdx6EDGLgjDXgsloWynd0h9ymUm3WvhruqI' alt='Chancellor Friedrich Merz attends a panel discussion with young people in Wuerzburg, southern Germany.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Work-hours context: Merz rejected the charge that the CDU sees Germans as lazy, saying he was pointing to Germany’s lower working time in international comparisons.</li><li>Domestic mood: Merz said people were too quick to view world affairs in “disaster mode” and urged them to remain optimistic despite multiple challenges.</li><li>Coalition criticism: At the same event, Merz said Germany’s governing coalition may argue too much and produce too few results, according to Kurier.</li><li>Democracy remarks: Merz defended democratic deliberation, saying dictatorships move faster but usually in the wrong direction, according to DW’s Turkish service and Kurier.</li></ul><blockquote>I would not recommend to my children today that they go to the U.S. to get an education and to work - Friedrich Merz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Friedrich Merz: Merz also discussed political culture, saying tolerance starts where one’s own opinion ends and asking whether Germans still listen to other views and seek common solutions. (<a href='https://www.diepresse.com/24173907/merz-wuerde-meinen-kindern-heute-nicht-empfehlen-in-die-usa-zu-gehen'>Die Presse</a>)</li><li>Süddeutsche Zeitung commentary: The paper asked whether a chancellor should make such a remark in passing and argued that Merz’s shifting communication on Trump could not continue. (<a href='https://www.sueddeutsche.de/meinung/bundeskanzler-merz-usa-kommunikation-berlin-kommentar-li.3484019'>Süddeutsche Zeitung</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/germanys-merz-wouldnt-advise-his-children-to-live-in-us/article70983574.ece'>Germany&#x27;s Merz ‘wouldn&#x27;t advise’ his children to live in U.S.</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/friedrich-merz-advises-against-us-study-work-social-climate/'>Merz wouldn’t tell his kids to move to America anymore</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-chancellor-merz-says-he-wouldn-t-advise-his-kids-to-go-to-the-us/live-77168830'>Germany news: Chancellor Merz says he wouldn&#x27;t advise his kids to go to the US</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/123f2c88-459a-4991-afb6-bbaa0dbe97fd'>Germany’s Merz says he would not want his children to move to US</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/wor